the target language is: lps.

% Spike: sale.l4's contract in LE for LPS (run with
% LPS_LE2_LIB=/work /lps2/lps run sale_lps.le).

% L4: PARTY Seller MUST deliver WITHIN 14
%       HENCE PARTY Buyer MUST pay 100 WITHIN 30 HENCE FULFILLED LEST BREACH BY Buyer BECAUSE "failed to pay"
%       LEST BREACH BY Seller BECAUSE "failed to deliver"

the maximum time is 60.

the events are:
    *a party* delivers; known as deliver.
    *a party* pays *an amount*; known as pay.

the actions are:
    *a party* breaches because *a reason*; known as breach.

the fluents are:
    *a party* must deliver by *a deadline*; known as must_deliver.
    *a party* must pay *an amount* by *a deadline*; known as must_pay.
    the sale is fulfilled; known as fulfilled.
    the sale is breached by *a party* because *a reason*; known as breached.

the knowledge base sale includes:

initially seller must deliver by 14.

when a party delivers from a first time to a second time
    and the party must deliver by a deadline
    and the first time =< the deadline
then it is not the case that the party must deliver by the deadline
    and buyer must pay 100 by the first time + 30.

when a party pays an amount
    and the party must pay a due amount by a deadline
    and the amount = the due amount
then it is not the case that the party must pay the due amount by the deadline
    and the sale is fulfilled.

if a party must deliver by a deadline at a first time
    and the first time > the deadline
then the party breaches because "failed to deliver" from the first time to a second time.

if a party must pay an amount by a deadline at a first time
    and the first time > the deadline
then the party breaches because "failed to pay" from the first time to a second time.

when a party breaches because a reason
then the sale is breached by the party because the reason
    and it is not the case that the party must deliver by a deadline.

when a party breaches because a reason
then it is not the case that the party must pay an amount by a deadline.

% One scenario per run: `lps run` ran the same history whichever scenario it
% was asked for. The other three histories of the spike, and the final state
% LPS2 reached with each (L4's answer in brackets):
%   seller delivers from 10 to 11, buyer pays 100 from 20 to 21
%       -> the sale is fulfilled                        (FULFILLED)
%   seller delivers from 10 to 11
%       -> buyer breaches because "failed to pay" at 42 (at 11: the payment
%          obligation with deadline 40 -- L4: PARTY Buyer MUST pay 100 WITHIN 30)
%   buyer pays 100 from 5 to 6
%       -> seller breaches because "failed to deliver" at 16

scenario late is:
    seller delivers from 10 to 11.
    buyer pays 100 from 50 to 51.
